-## Dense-matter references informing the implementation
-
-Current derivative audits and the cold analytic-electron repair are recorded
-in [DENSE_EOS.md](DENSE_EOS.md). They do not install a complete remnant EOS.
-
-[Skye (Jermyn et al. 2021)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.00691) provides a
-fully ionized free-energy EOS with Coulomb, mixture and quantum contributions
-and a self-consistent crystallization calculation. It does not replace a
-partially ionized atmospheric EOS.
-
-The [Ioffe electron-ion plasma source](https://www.ioffe.ru/astro/EIP/eipintr.html)
-provides another implementation and describes its quantum-liquid and mixture
-limitations. Its default classical melting criterion is an explicit source
-choice, which requires assessment for a helium-rich remnant.
-
-[Blouin, Dufour & Allard (2018)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.06616) demonstrate
-why the low-density approximations in ordinary stellar atmospheres are
-insufficient for dense, cool helium atmospheres. This motivates a distinct
-remnant boundary calculation; it does not establish coverage down to 100 K.
+# Physics and remaining work
+
+Ember uses one evolution program from Hayashi contraction through hydrogen
+burning and helium-white-dwarf cooling. The aim is to extend that calculation
+to extreme cold and conditional nucleon-decay scenarios.
+
+## Implemented
+
+- Deuterium, pp and CN burning, screening and plasma-neutrino losses.
+- Composition-dependent free-energy EOS, radiative opacity and electron conduction.
+- Non-gray gas atmospheres, including molecular collision-induced absorption.
+- Hot H/He/metal diffusion and conservative composition and energy transport.
+- Optional O/Ne/Fe/residual transport with EOS and conduction feedback.
+- Ledoux convection with instantaneous or finite-rate mixing. Fluid mixing
+  respects the selected solid phase; microscopic crystal diffusion is separate.
+- Adaptive time control, checked Richardson extrapolation and conservation audits.
+- A tested weakly quantum liquid-ion free-energy correction.
+- Optional crystallization and latent heat, with solid-ion mobility scenarios.
+  An H/He phase-equilibrium option also permits composition separation in
+  the dense, trace-metal envelope. A separate core option admits a liquid mixture
+  and pure-ion crystals.
+- Optional radioactive heating from a finite isotope inventory, including
+  daughter feeding and escaping neutrino energy.
+
+The grouped stellar calculation uses finite-rate mixing in hot convective
+regions and instantaneous mixing in the cooler envelope. Optional
+O/Ne/Fe/residual-metal transport feeds
+back on the EOS and conduction. Its separate update records, but does not
+supply, the associated change of material energy; metal-group gradients are
+not yet included in buoyancy. Rare elements remain diagnostic. The nuclear
+network is not full CNO.
+
+## Remaining work
+
+1. The fresh Hayashi sequence with active metal groups has cooled below 900 K.
+   Its metal-rich core uses a smooth liquid extension and separate pure-ion
+   crystals. Complete the sequence with consistent solver settings and measure
+   its end-to-end cost. Separate continuations reach 243.4 K; experimental
+   structures reach 196.2 K with additional proton-phase approximations.
+   These are not completed runs with the latest physics and numerics.
+   Finish conservative group accounting and energy coupling. Scaled profiles
+   and thermal projections are not substitutes for converged structures and ages.
+2. Validate molecular charge balance and nonideal atmosphere chemistry; extend
+   grain physics, cool diffusion, dense-matter thermodynamics
+   and conductive transport as the star enters their relevant regimes.
+3. Assess mixed crystals, element separation, crystal diffusion and strongly
+   quantum mixtures. The present core approximation uses separate pure crystals;
+   its transport currently stops all group flux where the bulk is solid, including
+   ions that could remain liquid. Resolve that limitation and individual-element
+   settling before interpreting central concentrations of rare heavy elements.
+4. Bound the effects of dense matter on weak-decay rates; assess condensed
+   hydrogen/helium surfaces, possible liquid layers and their
+   thermal boundary conditions before interpreting kelvin-scale cooling.
+5. At sufficiently low luminosity, compare isolation with accretion, tidal encounters
+   and conditional dark-matter heating. Treat nucleon decay with an explicit assumed
+   lifetime and retain stable baryons as an alternative.
+6. Extend the mass/composition survey across the stellar–brown-dwarf boundary.
+
+Some source tables remain local research inputs. The final physics has not yet
+been validated in one uninterrupted calculation. No extreme-cold cooling age
+or proton-decay track is claimed.
+
