 Full ionization is inappropriate in the cool envelope. `HotConduction` activates conductance with `w=u²(3−2u)` in log T between 3e5 and 1e6 K, then uses the full table. Below the interval the adopted conductance is zero (`kappa_cond=infinity`). Inside it, `kappa_cond` is divided by w and the derivative of w is included. This is an explicit domain join, not an ionization calculation. The high-temperature endpoint is chosen where H/He are effectively ionized in this star; metal ionization remains approximate.
 
 The static comparisons move that interval to 6e5..2e6 K and also remove conduction or change its source prescription. Their structural effect is below the relaxation accuracy for the sampled fully convective models. This does not justify omitting conduction after a radiative core develops. Full neutrino cooling, crystallization and dense-ion/remnant physics are separate unfinished inputs.
+
+## Cold interior and partially ionized envelope
+
+The temperature-only cutoff also removes conduction from cold dense matter,
+where pressure ionization can maintain mobile electrons. Its replacement
+requires a supported material domain. Electron degeneracy alone does not
+establish complete ionization, and the selected Helmholtz continuation does
+not supply a finite free-electron fraction for such a decision.
+
+The conductive fraction of diffusive heat flow is
+`kappa_rad / (kappa_rad + kappa_cond)`. A lower bound on radiative opacity
+cannot bound that fraction from above. With X = 0, Z = 0.02 and T = 200 kK,
+the selected radiative opacity at density 1 g/cm³ is 4.045e4 cm²/g; the
+fully ionized conductive value is 6172 cm²/g. Those inputs give a conductive
+fraction of 86.76%. At density 0.1 g/cm³ the conditional fraction is 13.34%.
+These comparisons show that the omitted interval cannot be dismissed using
+an opacity floor; they do not validate full ionization at those states.
+
+The [96-state comparison](results/cold_radiative_conductive_comparison_v1.json)
+retains 56 states with radiative source coverage and explicitly rejects the
+40 missing states. The cold helium EOS, pressure ionization and electron
+transport must be assessed together before replacing the selected cutoff.
