+## Physics and accuracy
+
+The selected code follows hydrogen, helium-3, carbon-12, carbon-13 and
+nitrogen-14 through burning, convection and microscopic transport; helium-4
+closes the baryonic mass. Carbon and nitrogen share the helium-group velocity.
+The material tables retain the fixed GS98 mixture, so individual catalyst
+settling and the changing carbon/nitrogen mixture's effect on material
+properties remain approximations. Oxygen branches and a new dense-plasma
+beryllium-capture prescription have not been selected. The current nuclear
+upper temperature limit is **20 MK**.
+
+At a retained **3.555 Tyr** structure, central hydrogen changes by
+**−1.723e-12 per year** through burning and **−2.626e-12 per year** through
+outward microscopic redistribution. Internal transport conserves total
+hydrogen. An independent hydrostatic estimate supports this transport scale;
+it does not establish the physical accuracy of every collision coefficient.
+[Hydrogen redistribution](HYDROGEN_REDISTRIBUTION.md).
+
+The relative composition tolerance is **0.001**. A matched **700.4 Myr**
+comparison changes luminosity by **0.001256%**, preserves the convective
+regions and uses **17.65%** less solver CPU. Full-step/two-half-step,
+structure, fuel and conservation checks remain active. A further tolerance
+increase to 0.003 was accurate over its tested interval but used **8.468%**
+more CPU and was rejected.
+
+The separate maximum abundance change per proposed interval is **0.005**.
+Its matched **1 Gyr** comparison reduces CPU by **24.99%**, with a
+**0.0005727%** luminosity difference and unchanged time and conservation
+criteria. This proposal guard is distinct from the relative composition
+error tolerance above.
+[Selection](results/abundance_step_guard_selection_v2.json).
+
+Four reusable threads share one set of EOS tables and retain exact collision
+responses. Their matched comparison preserves every physical output and
+reduces ordinary full-step/two-half-step CPU by **16.07%**. Reducing collision
+quadrature from 384/512 to 96/128 points saves a further **38.20%** of solver
+CPU in a matched **50 Myr** control, with coefficient, derivative and stellar
+checks passing. Reusing the two underlying electron-matrix derivatives
+saves 7.829% in a separate two-thread comparison. These timings describe the
+tested intervals, not a full-track runtime estimate. [Accuracy and computing time](PRODUCTION_ACCURACY.md).
+
+The selected two-metal collision approximation reduces solver CPU by
+**36.93%** in a 500 Myr comparison. Luminosity differs by **0.00006121%**,
+the largest absolute species difference is **8.868e-8**, and the convective
+regions and time/conservation checks agree. This approximates fixed collision
+carriers and static ion screening; the EOS and nuclear mixture remain intact.
+[Selection](results/two_metal_collision_selection_v1.json).
